course: dictionary
lesson: 2236
title: Cowrie: a shell that was money
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cowrie is a small sea snail, and the word can also mean its shell. Context tells you which meaning fits. «Cowrie shells were used as money for hundreds of years» shows that here the word means the shell.
explain: Read the words around the word. «Long ago, in parts of Africa and Asia, small shiny cowrie shells were used as money.» The clues are shiny and used as money. A shiny shell that people paid with must be the shell, not the snail.
explain: Other sentences add more clues. «People paid in grain, silver weighed on scales and cowrie shells» lists things people paid with, so cowries were valuable enough to trade for goods. Always check: does your meaning still make sense in the sentence?
fact: A cowrie is a small sea snail with a smooth, shiny, rounded shell.
fact: The shell of the cowrie snail was used as money long ago in parts of Africa, Asia and the Pacific.
fact: «Cowrie shells were used as money for hundreds of years» means people used them to buy and sell for a very long time.
fact: Cowrie shells are still used in jewellery today, such as «a string of cowries» in a necklace.
fact: Cowrie is also spelled cowry, and both spellings are correct.
exercise: «Long ago, in parts of Africa and Asia, small shiny cowrie shells were used as money.» What were cowrie shells used for here? | Building walls | Pets for children | *Money | Keeping warm at night
exercise: Which of these was NOT something people paid with, in «People paid in grain, silver weighed on scales and cowrie shells»? | Silver | *Pebbles | Cowrie shells | Grain
exercise: «Today a bucket of beach shells buys nothing.» What does this tell you? | Shells are sold in every shop | *Nobody agrees to use shells as money any more | Beach shells are used to pay taxes | Shells are rare and valuable today
test: What is a cowrie? | A kind of bird that lives on cliffs | A large fish that lives in rivers | *A small sea snail with a smooth, shiny shell | A grain used to make bread
test: «Long ago people used things that were hard to find and that everyone agreed on, like cowrie shells, salt, cattle and beads.» What does «hard to find» suggest? | Common, so they were free | Broken, so no one wanted them | *Rare, so they were worth a lot | Heavy, so they were hard to carry
exam: «Cowrie shells were used as money for hundreds of years in parts of Africa, India and China.» What does «used as money» tell you? | Cowries were food for children | Cowries were kept only in museums | Cowries were only worn as hats | *People used cowries to pay for things
